It's only got to go up and down.

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:04.590
Send Benny to the heliport now before the light goes.

00:41:07.660 --> 00:41:08.400
Where is everybody?

00:41:08.830 --> 00:41:10.110
Most of them are still trying to get back.

00:41:10.210 --> 00:41:11.370
The night staff haven't shown at all yet.

00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:13.190
If it's a heat mist, it can't last long.

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:16.400
If being the operative word, we've all seen a heat mist.

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:18.420
It rises a few feet above the ground in hot weather,

00:41:18.480 --> 00:41:19.980
but this one's four stories high.

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:23.140
And in two hours, it's virtually paralyzed a third of the globe.

00:41:23.960 --> 00:41:27.240
Look, France, Italy, here, and even India.

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:29.700
What sort of treatment, Jeff? Do we still link the bombs?

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:33.270
We do, and we go on linking them until someone up top proves how we're wrong.

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:34.860
I want something from you on this, Bill.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:36.220
Yes, I was afraid of that.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:37.050
Any angles?

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:37.790
Or a couple.

00:41:38.460 --> 00:41:42.060
This mist could be caused by an unusual amount of condensation

00:41:42.060 --> 00:41:44.610
from the unusual heat following all that rain.

00:41:45.340 --> 00:41:45.500
And that means...

00:41:46.140 --> 00:41:46.500
Alternatively...

00:41:46.620 --> 00:41:47.400
Alternatively what?

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:49.300
Well, this is just a personal guess.

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:50.480
I wouldn't dare to put it into print.

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:52.280
Let me decide what goes into print.

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:52.880
You just give.

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:56.240
It could be caused by a mass of extremely cold water

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:57.640
penetrating into the warmer currents.

00:41:57.800 --> 00:41:58.420
And what's that mean?

00:41:59.120 --> 00:41:59.980
An unusual amount of...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:03.920
melting ice at both poles well surely that would also mean floods they've already had them in

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:08.140
australia and new zealand are you telling me that the heat of the bombs melted the ice caps no sir

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:14.030
that wouldn't melt enough ice to flood the isle of white but if they did go off together supposing

00:42:14.030 --> 00:42:20.230
the combined thrust of the explosions shifted the tilt of the earth oh come on bill that would

00:42:20.230 --> 00:42:25.850
alter the climatic regions a complete change in the world's weather a new ice age for some new

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:29.920
New tropics, a new equator, I don't know what else it's all guesswork.

00:42:30.510 --> 00:42:31.670
It's all science fiction.

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:34.020
So were rockets to the moon and man satellites.

00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:35.340
We're going to have to move, sir.

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:38.700
Yes, right.

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:42.240
Miss Evans, all right, move and hit hard.

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:43.460
Bill, write your story.

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:44.980
I'm not sold on it, but I'll print it.

00:42:45.180 --> 00:42:46.300
It might force something big.

00:42:46.760 --> 00:42:48.620
Professor Tchaikovsky, just a minute, please.

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:49.020
Hold on.

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:49.780
We'll try it on him.

00:42:50.340 --> 00:42:51.460
Any quotes, you'll get them.

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:55.060
Professor Tchaikovsky, this is the Daily Express London.

00:42:56.820 --> 00:42:58.440
You're not getting any fancy flying.

00:42:58.600 --> 00:42:59.960
The deal is straight up, straight down.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:03.020
Well, don't think this isn't my favorite assignment, because it isn't.

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:15.100
What about a look at London Airport?

00:43:15.640 --> 00:43:16.800
Something's just impossible.

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:19.840
My orders are everything's possible, even the Indian rope tree.

00:43:20.110 --> 00:43:21.350
You may need that to get down.

00:43:25.210 --> 00:43:29.030
All flight departures have been delayed for another hour.

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:33.620
be obtained from the information desk opposite channel 7.

00:43:34.270 --> 00:43:37.530
London approach, speed bird 352, leaving Watford.

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:41.520
Roger 352, descend to 2,000 feet on your present heading.

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:42.900
2,000 feet, roger.

00:43:43.740 --> 00:43:46.940
Sorry KLM 603, have to return you, priority transatlantic only.

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:50.400
KLM 603, what is the Amsterdam weather?

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:53.120
Hello 603, visibility 50 yards in fog.

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:59.390
Roger 352.

00:44:00.030 --> 00:44:02.170
The runway visibility now is 80 yards.

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:33.420
Oh, no.

00:44:34.810 --> 00:44:36.710
OK, Colonel, let down the drawbridge.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:40.520
You've been to Fleet Street already, sir?

00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:44.020
I've been on a hike where all streets turn left and all roads lead to home.

00:44:44.440 --> 00:44:47.240
Fortunately, I found this in a sand dune just past Benghazi.

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:48.200
It's getting hotter, too.

00:44:48.570 --> 00:44:49.310
91 degrees.

00:44:49.510 --> 00:44:50.510
It's going to be quite a summer.

00:44:51.090 --> 00:44:52.410
Well, let us share a toast, Colonel.

00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:57.110
I give you this battered and benumbed world of sweating moles and radio static.

00:44:57.390 --> 00:44:58.070
You can have it.

00:44:58.380 --> 00:44:59.480
Oh, thanks very much, sir.

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:08.870
I interrupt this program to bring you a further bulleted mist, which is hit likely to increase in density for the next...

00:45:09.690 --> 00:45:10.670
And the best of British luck.

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:14.750
The police have asked the public to keep the roads for essential...

00:45:15.610 --> 00:45:19.420
...and avoid traffic congestion by arranging where possible to stay where they are.

00:45:19.810 --> 00:45:21.810
I think our British police are wonderful. Where's the houseboat?

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:22.950
Over there, sir.

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:25.220
We return you now to late night dance.

00:45:33.740 --> 00:45:57.960
Guess who? Clever girl. Were you asleep?

00:45:58.480 --> 00:45:59.760
No, I was just washing my hair.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:00.820
Did you make it all right?

00:46:01.100 --> 00:46:02.460
I certainly did. I'm back downstairs.

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:05.620
Well, it's a two-hour story. You're listening to the radio.

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:10.370
Did you hear that bulletin? So, you pro-police or anti-police?

00:46:12.370 --> 00:46:15.710
Yes, yes, I'm still with you. I was just trying to work out all the permutations.

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:17.110
There's only two of them.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:20.860
You can send me out into the cold, cold snow and let me get lost with the rest of the kids,

00:46:20.900 --> 00:46:23.860
or you can utter two one-syllable words and become a law-abiding citizen.

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:25.330
Can you be one, too?

00:46:27.400 --> 00:46:29.980
Well, since this thing seems to be bigger than both of us...

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:05.100
I'm beginning to think you arranged this.

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:08.620
But of course, you look cute, sort of boyish.

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:10.920
And we'll just remember you're normal.

00:47:11.920 --> 00:47:12.100
Yes.

00:47:13.650 --> 00:47:15.310
Well, this is all very sweet and spontaneous.

00:47:15.450 --> 00:47:16.490
I don't mind sleeping on the couch.

00:47:16.690 --> 00:47:17.470
I haven't got a couch.

00:47:17.920 --> 00:47:19.580
Oh, in that case, I'll just cuddle up on the floor

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:21.220
at the bottom of your bed like a faithful sub-burner.

00:47:21.320 --> 00:47:21.980
No, you won't.

00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:23.960
Can't we just have a flaming fog flare between us?

00:47:24.080 --> 00:47:24.800
Won't be necessary.

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:26.340
If you're staying, you're sleeping in here.

00:47:26.460 --> 00:47:27.940
I've got a feeling you've been through all this before.

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:28.640
I have not.

00:47:28.780 --> 00:47:29.980
You mean I'm the first man you've managed to...

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:33.000
You'd be perfectly comfortable with a pillow and a couple of sheets.

00:47:33.180 --> 00:47:33.560
Excuse me.

00:47:34.450 --> 00:47:36.050
Well, supposing you want to use the bathroom in the night?

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:38.110
We'll deal with each problem as we get to it.

00:47:43.850 --> 00:47:47.350
I have a big problem, Jeannie. As well as being normal, I'm human.

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:49.820
You're also a pushover, Pete.

00:47:51.170 --> 00:47:51.910
Meaning you're not interested?

00:47:53.080 --> 00:47:53.880
Yeah, I could be.

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:56.700
Don't make it so easy. It'd be hard to get.

00:47:56.860 --> 00:47:57.960
Make me fight for you.

00:48:01.370 --> 00:48:02.470
It's an unfair contest.

00:48:03.200 --> 00:48:03.700
Who knows?

00:48:03.940 --> 00:48:05.040
You might even be lovable.

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:06.780
But don't rush me.

00:48:06.860 --> 00:48:08.260
I'm allergic to one-night stands.

00:48:08.940 --> 00:48:10.620
Now, if you'll give me five minutes, I'll finish my hair.

00:48:10.740 --> 00:48:12.680
Normally, I'd offer to help, but in the circumstances...

00:48:12.680 --> 00:48:15.140
In the circumstances, you can take a well-earned nightcap.

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:16.740
There should be a bottle of something in the kitchen.

00:48:17.380 --> 00:48:17.700
I forgot.

00:48:17.860 --> 00:48:18.880
St. Bernard's carry their own.

00:48:19.350 --> 00:48:20.490
You'll get a glass in there, too.

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:21.210
Maybe I will.

00:48:21.430 --> 00:48:28.700
Maybe I won't.

00:48:36.490 --> 00:48:36.930
Did you find it?

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:38.100
Yes, I found it.

00:48:39.320 --> 00:48:41.880
Look, if I've got a play hard to get for you to go steady with me,

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:43.460
how about a few immigration details?

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:44.540
Such as?

00:48:44.860 --> 00:48:46.900
Oh, where you slept for the last seven years?

00:48:47.360 --> 00:48:50.260
Have you ever suffered from marriage, divorce or any similar virus?

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:52.170
Only two near infections.

00:48:52.660 --> 00:48:53.640
Any other inquiries?

00:48:54.490 --> 00:48:55.610
No, the rest seems to be all right.

00:49:00.350 --> 00:49:00.770
Maybe I will.

00:49:05.250 --> 00:49:06.050
One or two pillows.

00:49:06.270 --> 00:49:07.750
Oh, I'll just wedge my head between the taps.

00:49:07.950 --> 00:49:09.930
I'd offer you a pair of my pajamas, but it's a bit hot.

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:13.500
You wouldn't like to come into my place for a drink?

00:49:13.970 --> 00:49:14.890
It's against the house rules.

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:15.870
Yeah, you'd like to get sober.

00:49:16.950 --> 00:49:17.630
Like you sober.

00:49:27.440 --> 00:49:27.660
Pete?

00:49:29.490 --> 00:49:29.970
For the use of the...

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:30.710
Welcome.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:36.640
Well...

00:50:20.250 --> 00:50:20.470
Hello?

00:50:22.690 --> 00:50:23.430
This club?

00:50:24.980 --> 00:50:25.640
Oh, yes.

00:50:26.010 --> 00:50:26.490
Just a minute.

00:50:27.630 --> 00:50:27.970
Pee!

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:29.840
Oh!

00:50:30.890 --> 00:50:32.490
So what do we do now? Can you push it under the door?

00:50:33.080 --> 00:50:35.780
Come and get it, idiot. Just a minute, please.

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:39.160
Sorry, it must be the office.

00:50:42.660 --> 00:50:43.460
Yes, standing here.

00:50:44.110 --> 00:50:46.930
We just put the first edition to bed without you. I hope that's all right.

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:50.360
Okay, okay. It caught you there, it caught me here. There's no justice.

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:53.600
But just to even things up, you can crawl round to the Met Centre tomorrow morning

00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:56.440
and get me some comparative ice flu figures for the last 20 years.

00:50:56.960 --> 00:50:59.980
You'd be surprised at what I might bring you tomorrow. I've made a new...

00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:04.240
Uh-huh. Well, they're rather fussy about new members.

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:06.780
You have to be vouched for by two friends, and you don't have two friends.

00:51:08.130 --> 00:51:12.050
Yeah, you too, you vulgar man. Compromise while telephoning.

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:13.520
What does it all mean, Pete?

00:51:13.620 --> 00:51:14.700
It means you'll probably have to marry me.

00:51:15.140 --> 00:51:17.460
I mean the mist and this crazy weather. What is it?

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:19.720
Don't ask me. You know more about what's going on than I do.

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:20.720
Oh, but I don't.

00:51:20.860 --> 00:51:24.220
You've been running around that office for months trying to hide signs of the elephants of past.

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:26.460
I don't understand half the things I hear anyway.

00:51:26.560 --> 00:51:28.710
Such as. I'm no mastermind, but such as.

00:51:32.490 --> 00:51:35.180
Be fair. I'm not even supposed to be talking to you.

00:51:36.130 --> 00:51:37.270
So far, that's all you've done.

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:40.430
Let's change the subject, shall we?

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:40.830
To what?

00:51:41.540 --> 00:51:42.120
Pete's Denning.

00:51:42.260 --> 00:51:42.620
Too dull.

00:51:42.840 --> 00:51:43.560
What happened, Pete?

00:51:43.780 --> 00:51:44.340
What, what happened?

00:51:45.510 --> 00:51:46.690
They say you used to be a writer.

00:51:48.830 --> 00:51:51.430
Look, let's just put that subject on the same list as the weather, shall we?

00:51:51.980 --> 00:51:53.620
Well, that leaves us with nothing to talk about.

00:51:55.480 --> 00:51:56.160
Do we have to talk?

00:51:56.900 --> 00:51:57.600
No, but seriously.

00:51:57.780 --> 00:51:58.800
I'm not unserious, Jeannie.

00:52:03.680 --> 00:52:07.540
Look, Jeannie, I don't want to make love to you.

00:52:08.060 --> 00:52:10.130
I don't want to hold you or kiss you

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:12.540
because you don't appeal to me one little bit.

00:52:13.950 --> 00:52:15.050
But please fight for me, Jeannie.

00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:31.670
I think it will last a long time.

00:52:31.930 --> 00:52:32.370
The fog?

00:52:32.660 --> 00:52:32.960
Us.

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:35.720
Why do women always worry about the end right at the beginning?

00:52:36.200 --> 00:52:36.840
I'm not women.

00:52:37.020 --> 00:52:37.760
I mean...

00:52:37.760 --> 00:52:38.560
I know what you mean.

00:52:38.940 --> 00:52:39.880
So move over then.

00:54:21.080 --> 00:54:23.380
shall be found out for the last

00:54:23.660 --> 00:54:26.510
judgment. With thee, repent!

00:54:46.570 --> 00:54:47.790
Good morning, Sarge. Anyone in yet?

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:50.870
Oh, a few. Most of the night duty staff have only just left.

00:54:51.030 --> 00:54:52.630
Cost a fortune in overtime, it will.

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:54.290
Well, there you are, boy. Just relax and get rich.

00:54:54.430 --> 00:54:57.090
On the left side of order. In the power field it blew a fuse or something.

00:54:57.290 --> 00:54:58.810
I'm liable to do the same in this heat.

00:55:11.960 --> 00:55:13.400
Another cyclone, in Greece.

00:55:13.600 --> 00:55:14.500
Another one or the same one?

00:55:14.890 --> 00:55:16.090
The casualties aren't quite so high.

00:55:16.850 --> 00:55:18.830
Boy, Greece, Italy, France and us.

00:55:19.660 --> 00:55:21.120
Still along the old man's same line.

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:23.250
Mr. Maguire, I ask you to follow it through.

00:55:23.630 --> 00:55:23.790
Yes, sir.

00:55:27.770 --> 00:55:29.670
Jackabee says, will you follow this through, Mr. Maguire?

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:30.980
Can you send any Benzadrine?

00:55:31.700 --> 00:55:32.020
Benzadrine?

00:55:32.180 --> 00:55:33.700
Ah, Skibbert, give me a cold drink, will you?

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:35.440
If I'm asleep when you fetch it, nudge me.

00:55:35.830 --> 00:55:36.170
Yes, sir.

00:55:36.270 --> 00:55:36.870
I can't hear you.

00:55:37.840 --> 00:55:40.100
If Le Bourget is out, try one of the private flying clubs

00:55:40.100 --> 00:55:41.620
or get them by road to Brussels.

00:55:41.860 --> 00:55:42.800
We've got to have those pictures.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:44.640
Yes, they'll get a credit.

00:55:45.410 --> 00:55:47.940
No, I don't want the small roads.

00:55:48.060 --> 00:55:49.020
It's just the main highway diversion.

00:55:49.960 --> 00:55:50.980
Harry Match have got pictures.

00:55:51.080 --> 00:55:51.740
Is it across the same?

00:55:52.060 --> 00:55:52.680
When do we get them?

00:55:52.870 --> 00:55:53.690
A couple of hours with luck.

00:55:54.130 --> 00:55:55.150
It almost wrecked Le Bourget.

00:55:55.580 --> 00:55:56.660
The other airports are jammed.

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:57.940
We'll need a photo news expert.

00:55:58.340 --> 00:55:59.460
Hope nobody kicks about the price.

00:56:00.250 --> 00:56:03.100
For the news, Bill, there's an extra page going out.

00:56:03.140 --> 00:56:04.720
Right, Jacko, like the old British days, isn't it?

00:56:04.800 --> 00:56:06.020
Where the hell were you all yesterday?

00:56:06.260 --> 00:56:08.200
You may remember there was a mist that covered three quarters.

00:56:08.220 --> 00:56:09.700
You may remember that was in the afternoon.

00:56:09.800 --> 00:56:12.020
I wasn't drunk and I wasn't drinking, if that's what you're suggesting.

00:56:12.140 --> 00:56:13.920
I'm suggesting wherever you were, you'd be more use here.

00:56:14.080 --> 00:56:16.660
If so happens, I made a new contact at the Met Centre yesterday.

00:56:16.660 --> 00:56:17.180
Anything usable?

00:56:17.750 --> 00:56:20.560
Not yet. Anyway, Bill Maguire knew where to reach for your day.

00:56:20.620 --> 00:56:22.180
Unfortunately, Bill Maguire doesn't pay you, Peter.

00:56:23.700 --> 00:56:24.340
No, sir.

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:27.260
Hello, Mr. Senning. What about that cycle?

00:56:27.460 --> 00:56:29.000
First one ever in Europe. Did you know that?

00:56:29.080 --> 00:56:29.800
I do now, thank you.

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:31.160
They had a whirlwind in Greece, too.

00:56:31.240 --> 00:56:32.980
Give me that, or you'll have one right up your backside.

00:56:33.340 --> 00:56:33.380
Sorry, sir. Come on.

00:56:33.500 --> 00:56:34.640
It's not very cold. The fridge pack.

00:56:34.640 --> 00:56:35.220
You been here all night?

00:56:35.320 --> 00:56:38.060
Oh, don't ask. I'm still recovering. Any damage up your end?

00:56:38.140 --> 00:56:39.640
A few windows broken. It just skirted us.

00:56:39.820 --> 00:56:41.260
Still, at least it blew the mist away.

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:42.320
Why don't you blow yourself?

00:56:42.500 --> 00:56:44.780
Oh, don't sort the kid out. The temperature's not his fault.

00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:45.780
Yes, it's not my fault.

00:56:45.820 --> 00:56:46.360
Oh, shut up.

00:56:46.500 --> 00:56:47.340
All right, all right.

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:49.430
Why does the whole world have to drink this stuff?

00:56:49.430 --> 00:56:51.450
I understand there was organized chaos here last night.

00:56:51.590 --> 00:56:53.710
The old man put the addition forward one hour to beat the fog.

00:56:53.790 --> 00:56:56.210
Comes the cyclone to complete change-up with only half stop.

00:56:56.370 --> 00:56:57.630
Oh, it's bloody hot.

00:56:58.290 --> 00:56:59.790
Ninety-eight degrees on the ammunition.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:02.860
Think of all those wafts stripped to the fuselage. What's this?

00:57:03.180 --> 00:57:05.520
Library clips. Cyclones, typhoons, hurricanes.

00:57:05.680 --> 00:57:08.620
It's all there. West Pacific, Northern Australia, Madagascar, Bay of Bengal.

00:57:09.080 --> 00:57:12.740
List the figures for me, will you? Rate of advance, acceleration and casualties.

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:16.720
Why don't I just do 500 steaming words on how mankind is so full of wind

00:57:16.720 --> 00:57:17.920
it's about to outblown nature.

00:57:18.220 --> 00:57:19.820
Fine, but after you've done my figures.

00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:21.230
Yes, of course.

00:57:22.010 --> 00:57:23.530
Oh, give me those ice float figures, will you?

00:57:23.960 --> 00:57:24.440
Ice float?

00:57:25.510 --> 00:57:26.810
You mean you didn't go to the Met Center?

00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:29.210
Oh, those. Yes, I went. I dropped Jeannie off.

00:57:29.350 --> 00:57:29.930
All right, all right.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:32.200
She's the new contact. Works there. She's calling them through.

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:34.400
You, of course, were too busy to get them.

00:57:34.870 --> 00:57:36.450
She only has to look up the files.

00:57:36.590 --> 00:57:38.350
I thought it more important to be here this morning.

00:57:38.510 --> 00:57:39.090
Not to me, it isn't.

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:42.830
Apparently. Well, you'll be glad to hear I've just about had the whole issue.

00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:44.580
That's up to you, chum.

00:57:48.490 --> 00:57:51.980
Okay, so the knife's penetrated deep. Can I go now?

00:57:53.760 --> 00:57:56.600
Ah, sit down, Pete. Give her a ring.

00:57:56.700 --> 00:57:58.040
Our switchboard's probably jammed.

00:58:03.540 --> 00:58:08.200
Can I speak to you for a moment, Sandy?

00:58:08.320 --> 00:58:09.160
Oh, not now, Pete.

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:09.840
It won't take a minute.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:12.600
Look, all I want to do is to finish this lot and get some sleep.

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:16.420
I want to turn it in.

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:17.640
Do what?

00:58:17.720 --> 00:58:19.000
Turn in the job. Jack it in. Resign.

00:58:19.420 --> 00:58:20.240
Well, I can't stop it.

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:22.760
Look, I am sick of being the ass-end of Bill Maguire's donkey.

00:58:23.020 --> 00:58:24.300
Why didn't you leave it till the temperature drops?

00:58:24.500 --> 00:58:24.960
We're all a little early.

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:26.200
Well, I'm like a cub reporter here.

00:58:26.320 --> 00:58:28.660
Phone up for figures, run down to the library, take these pictures.

00:58:28.760 --> 00:58:29.220
What am I?

00:58:29.300 --> 00:58:29.980
Are you getting paid for it?

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:33.940
Look, it's not so long ago. This paper used to put up posters telling the public to read my stuff.

00:58:34.100 --> 00:58:35.800
You stop writing it, so how could we go on vindicate?

00:58:36.760 --> 00:58:39.640
Look, Pete, I'll forget you resigned. Give it a few days.

00:58:39.920 --> 00:58:41.560
Anyway, I thought Bill McGuire was a friend of yours.

00:58:42.910 --> 00:58:44.740
You always behave like a friend, Sarnison.

00:58:44.980 --> 00:58:46.040
Yeah, maybe it's the heat.

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:52.240
For you, think about it.

00:58:53.940 --> 00:58:55.770
Yeah, I will. Stenning, who's that?

00:58:56.240 --> 00:58:59.820
It's Jeanie. Pete, I've got to talk to you. Serious.

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:02.470
I can't. Not on the phone. I've got to see you.

00:59:02.830 --> 00:59:06.680
You will tonight, remember? Well, if it's that urgent, hop a cab and come here.

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:10.470
Can't. I'm in a telephone box and I've got to get back.

00:59:10.990 --> 00:59:15.080
Listen, I'll take an early lunch. 12.30 Battersea Park, the restaurant by the Boating Lake.

00:59:15.720 --> 00:59:17.750
All right, Jeanie. Jane?

00:59:18.490 --> 00:59:20.970
Oh, yeah, it's a new contact I've found at the Met Office.

00:59:21.110 --> 00:59:22.650
She seems to have something on her mind.

00:59:22.790 --> 00:59:24.340
Met Office? Has she got a story?

00:59:24.500 --> 00:59:26.160
Well, frankly, I don't think she'd notice a news story.

00:59:26.260 --> 00:59:28.300
If it was in 72 points still, I don't think we should ignore it.

00:59:28.420 --> 00:59:29.860
Well, she's your contact. All right.

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:32.620
But you're my witness for the defense when Stenning's found missing again.

00:59:32.820 --> 00:59:33.760
What are you, cub reporter?

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:34.640
Come on, get out.

00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:45.920
Of all people.

00:59:46.280 --> 00:59:47.760
It must be three hours since we met.

00:59:47.940 --> 00:59:49.080
There's too many people here.

00:59:49.550 --> 00:59:50.130
Who's shy?

00:59:50.270 --> 00:59:50.930
Only want to kiss you.

00:59:52.420 --> 00:59:54.080
Pete, I mustn't be seen talking.

00:59:54.180 --> 00:59:55.420
I know, you're a Russian spy.

00:59:55.640 --> 00:59:56.800
All right, I'll come to Moscow with you.

00:59:57.120 --> 00:59:58.720
Oh, Pete, please be serious.

00:59:59.120 --> 00:59:59.760
Never more so.

01:00:00.440 --> 01:00:03.500
but you don't understand so you're going to tell me now get in the carriage go on

01:00:03.500 --> 01:00:12.640
no expense spared with stennings well what could be more private

01:00:20.340 --> 01:00:29.700
take your time i bought a double ride you got a cigarette sure this morning

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:35.350
of the girls didn't get in so some of us had to go on the switchboard again and i've overheard

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:40.260
things before sometimes you can't help it but you keep it to yourself the switchboard is very

01:00:40.260 --> 01:00:46.140
confidential especially a government but this morning you've got to promise me you won't use

01:00:46.140 --> 01:00:52.950
this is it about the big question mark and how can i promise you must oh for god's sake jeannie

01:00:53.100 --> 01:00:56.360
if something's gone wrong don't you think people are entitled to know i think the people

01:00:56.360 --> 01:00:59.960
at the top are cleverer than we are they know what they're doing then why tell me because i

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:01.400
I had to tell someone or bust.

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:03.060
I thought you were the one person I could tell.

01:01:03.220 --> 01:01:03.780
Maybe I was wrong.

01:01:03.860 --> 01:01:05.080
No, you weren't wrong, Jeannie.

01:01:05.980 --> 01:01:06.380
Now, go on.

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:10.190
I didn't understand very much of it,

01:01:10.270 --> 01:01:12.660
but what I did understand,

01:01:12.900 --> 01:01:14.340
I don't know, I panicked and called you.

01:01:14.420 --> 01:01:14.960
So here I am.

01:01:15.060 --> 01:01:17.830
I want your promise.

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:29.970
All right.

01:01:30.940 --> 01:01:33.200
May, two hot lagers for the daily sketch.

01:01:33.480 --> 01:01:35.080
You're lucky I've worked two nice ones on ice.

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:36.900
You know you haven't because I've just sold them.

01:01:37.080 --> 01:01:39.460
I told you to hang onto those in case Mr. McGuire will do them.

01:01:39.600 --> 01:01:40.300
Never heard you.

01:01:40.400 --> 01:01:42.840
You never hear anything. You can't keep ice in this weather.

01:01:43.160 --> 01:01:45.020
No, three hot scotches. Good for the circulation.

01:01:45.540 --> 01:01:46.620
A personal, not professional.

01:01:47.020 --> 01:01:48.420
You are, Mr. McGuire. Thanks, May.

01:01:50.420 --> 01:01:52.260
I need you, Dad, and you need that, so drink it.

01:01:52.420 --> 01:01:53.420
Put them on the slate, may we?

01:01:57.340 --> 01:01:59.320
I heard a funny story on my way to the park this morning.

01:01:59.460 --> 01:01:59.880
How funny.

01:02:01.790 --> 01:02:03.030
What's the nutation of the Earth?

01:02:03.450 --> 01:02:07.290
Nutation? Well, it's a slight oscillation on the Earth's axis,

01:02:07.630 --> 01:02:09.490
caused by the pull of the sun and the moon on the equator.

01:02:10.090 --> 01:02:12.110
You see, there's a slight bulge on the...

01:02:12.930 --> 01:02:15.110
There's also an item here about axis rotation.

01:02:15.270 --> 01:02:17.210
There's been 11-degree variation, whatever that may mean.

01:02:17.460 --> 01:02:18.140
Where'd this come from?

01:02:18.240 --> 01:02:19.720
Never mind where it came from. Translate it.

01:02:20.240 --> 01:02:21.080
It means I was right.

01:02:21.570 --> 01:02:22.130
Now, congratulations.

01:02:23.320 --> 01:02:24.740
They've shifted the tilt of the Earth.

01:02:26.090 --> 01:02:28.770
The stupid, crazy, irresponsible bastards.

01:02:31.150 --> 01:02:32.110
They've finally done it.

01:02:36.630 --> 01:02:37.770
That's the normal angle of tilt.

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:41.750
An 11 degree variation would put it this way or there.

01:02:42.770 --> 01:02:44.170
We don't know whether it's east or west.

01:02:44.370 --> 01:02:44.650
Nope.

01:02:45.220 --> 01:02:47.440
Your weather line theory would indicate an east to west tilt.

01:02:48.120 --> 01:02:49.220
Where'd you get this story, Stanley?

01:02:49.400 --> 01:02:50.820
I'd prefer you not to ask, sir.

01:02:51.700 --> 01:02:54.220
Get me Sir John Kelly, either at his office or at his home.

01:02:54.530 --> 01:02:55.770
Wherever he is, I want to talk to him.

01:02:55.830 --> 01:02:56.830
Does this come from your new contact?

01:02:56.870 --> 01:02:57.910
Never mind where it comes from.

01:02:57.990 --> 01:02:58.790
You take my word.

01:02:58.910 --> 01:02:59.570
That's the story.

01:03:00.280 --> 01:03:03.180
What can this do to us, Bill, apart from altering the Earth's climates?

01:03:03.280 --> 01:03:05.470
Well, monkeying around with nature on the scale.

01:03:05.780 --> 01:03:07.180
Who knows what the implications are?

01:03:07.380 --> 01:03:08.360
Well, what do you think?

01:03:08.500 --> 01:03:10.010
I prefer not to.

01:03:11.630 --> 01:03:14.170
Yes? I didn't ask for the PRO.

01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:16.400
All right, put him through.

01:03:16.580 --> 01:03:16.880
Who is it?

01:03:17.040 --> 01:03:17.500
Holroyd, sir.

01:03:18.240 --> 01:03:19.860
Get Jacko in. Get everybody in.

01:03:20.480 --> 01:03:23.590
Holroyd, this is Jefferson, the editor of the Daily Express here.

01:03:24.900 --> 01:03:28.040
Yes, well, you can tell Sir John that he has the choice of being disturbed now

01:03:28.040 --> 01:03:29.850
or when he reads his morning paper.

01:03:30.320 --> 01:03:32.140
We're going to print whether he talks or not.

01:03:32.890 --> 01:03:33.690
Bill, get moving.

01:03:34.040 --> 01:03:36.640
I want a pictorial panorama of the world as it's going to be

01:03:36.640 --> 01:03:38.580
with the new climatic zones and all the rest of it change.

01:03:38.980 --> 01:03:40.260
Steny, come in on this phone.

01:03:40.540 --> 01:03:41.100
Take this down.

01:03:41.960 --> 01:03:43.520
Sir John Kelly, Jefferson here.

01:03:44.030 --> 01:03:45.830
I'd like a statement and I'd like it now.

01:03:47.030 --> 01:03:48.030
Nothing's impossible.

01:03:48.360 --> 01:03:51.120
Not even an 11-degree tilt in the globe's axis.

01:03:52.210 --> 01:03:54.470
Yes, that's precisely what we're going to print.

01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:08.540
Morning, sir. What about all this then? World tips over. Fog, cyclone, and flood.

01:05:09.260 --> 01:05:23.330
Jeannie, what else could I do?

01:05:23.330 --> 01:05:24.490
I don't want to hear. I don't want to know.

01:05:24.490 --> 01:05:27.230
It had to come up, Jeannie. You couldn't sit on a thing like this.

01:05:27.410 --> 01:05:28.130
That's right. That's right.

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:29.980
But we didn't use your name, Jeannie.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:31.380
Nobody used your name.

01:05:31.540 --> 01:05:32.520
Who cares about my name?

01:05:32.620 --> 01:05:33.280
My name's nothing.

01:05:33.440 --> 01:05:34.480
It's my trust you've used.

01:05:35.260 --> 01:05:35.560
All right.

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:35.940
I'm ready.

01:05:37.350 --> 01:05:37.890
Who are you?

01:05:38.230 --> 01:05:39.370
I'm a security officer.

01:05:39.510 --> 01:05:39.890
Now, wait a minute.

01:05:39.990 --> 01:05:40.690
Where are you taking her?

01:05:40.830 --> 01:05:42.510
For the moment, into preventative custody.

01:05:42.770 --> 01:05:42.970
For what?

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:44.010
Can we go now, please?

01:05:44.830 --> 01:05:45.770
What the hell are you preventing?

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:46.690
It's happened, man.

01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:50.350
You've got the head weathercock sitting on the biggest addled name the human race has ever laid.

01:05:50.430 --> 01:05:51.150
Leave it alone, please.

01:05:51.250 --> 01:05:51.970
It's none of your business.

01:05:52.310 --> 01:05:55.710
They'll let these bloody bombs on as though they were fallen scribes on bonfire night

01:05:55.710 --> 01:05:56.790
and you want their people a secret.

01:05:57.680 --> 01:05:58.420
Fine, fine.

01:05:58.520 --> 01:05:59.980
So they stick the kid into preventative custody.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:02.480
Oh, cool off. I know it's hot, but cool off.

01:06:02.480 --> 01:06:05.760
Never mind the data on leukemia or infant mortality or strontium G

01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:07.780
or any of the other clinical facts of mass suicide.

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:09.820
Just stick some kid in a cell and everything will be all right.

01:06:09.980 --> 01:06:11.320
Go and write about it just like that.

01:06:11.440 --> 01:06:14.880
Oh, sure. Now they want to read about the filthy, self-destructive force

01:06:14.880 --> 01:06:18.640
humanity carries around rotting in its belly now when it's too late.

01:06:18.820 --> 01:06:20.840
It's never too late for a good story well written.

01:06:20.940 --> 01:06:23.160
The human race has been poisoning itself for years

01:06:23.160 --> 01:06:25.400
with a great big smile on its fat face.

01:06:25.620 --> 01:06:26.640
Well, that's how it is, Pete.

01:06:26.860 --> 01:06:29.040
People don't care about the news until it becomes personal.

01:06:29.100 --> 01:06:29.980
Well, it has become personal.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:36.320
for me isn't there anything you can do what else is there to do i've made me will look pete they're

