Yeah, they grabbed 5,000 gallons.

01:13:34.000 --> 01:13:36.000
Marvellous, isn't it? I can remember when it used to be money.

01:13:36.640 --> 01:13:38.320
I must say, this is all a bit frightening.

01:13:38.560 --> 01:13:40.260
Nothing to worry about unless you own a reservoir.

01:13:40.720 --> 01:13:41.520
No, I meant the job.

01:13:41.680 --> 01:13:42.580
Oh, you'll get numb to it.

01:13:43.020 --> 01:13:44.600
The chief librarian's a nice guy.

01:13:44.740 --> 01:13:45.460
He'll put you right.

01:13:45.620 --> 01:13:47.400
If there's any special problem, there's always me.

01:13:48.110 --> 01:13:48.510
Or Pete.

01:13:49.880 --> 01:13:52.340
Everything's filed here from the atom bomb to send Buddhism.

01:13:53.010 --> 01:13:55.730
Bill, I appreciate the motives, but don't try and restart anything.

01:13:55.870 --> 01:13:56.890
It's very, very over.

01:13:57.070 --> 01:13:57.970
A breakdown, Jeannie.

01:13:58.070 --> 01:13:59.910
What do you want him in the penitence parade, walking Beth?

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:08.260
footed down Fleet Street. I don't want him anywhere. This is no time to waste time. Well, he isn't wasting it. He's ours on a date. In Battersea Park with his kid.

01:14:09.360 --> 01:14:29.010
Oh, Miss Craig. You found your way, eh? Mr. McGuire brought me. Yes, I shouldn't think we'd ever be lonely down here now. Look after her, Archie. She's a bright girl. It's just a sense of proportion that's a bit bent. Here we are. Eight shillings worth of deliciously hot cocoa. Take that to Miss Craig, will you?

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:30.440
What's the matter, kid?

01:14:32.220 --> 01:14:32.960
I'm all right, sir.

01:14:33.880 --> 01:14:35.420
Holy smoke, look at these temperatures.

01:14:35.900 --> 01:14:38.740
Mexico Delta, 145.6 degrees Fahrenheit.

01:14:39.140 --> 01:14:41.300
USA, Texas, 141.3.

01:14:42.550 --> 01:14:43.190
Rome, 139.

01:14:43.330 --> 01:14:45.130
This famine death count's pretty terrifying.

01:14:45.150 --> 01:14:46.550
I just don't get this lack of water.

01:14:47.060 --> 01:14:48.840
What about all this extra polar ice that's melting?

01:14:49.000 --> 01:14:50.260
Well, the heat state can get up as paper.

01:14:50.540 --> 01:14:52.240
Yeah, but doesn't it have to come down again sometimes?

01:14:52.520 --> 01:14:54.740
It has, and when it does, pray you're on high ground.

01:15:03.420 --> 01:15:05.260
Dave, I just came from Battersea Park.

01:15:05.340 --> 01:15:06.420
Personally, I prefer Brighton.

01:15:07.060 --> 01:15:09.020
Look, something's going on. If you're not interested, to hell with it.

01:15:09.180 --> 01:15:12.380
Unwind, for God's sake. How do I know if I'm interested? What sort of something?

01:15:12.770 --> 01:15:15.210
Well, they're putting up long sheds, you know, the army type.

01:15:15.450 --> 01:15:17.890
And there's trucks full of piping and shower bath fittings.

01:15:18.250 --> 01:15:20.790
500-gallon water tanks, complete with purifying systems.

01:15:20.970 --> 01:15:21.270
Battersea Park.

01:15:21.410 --> 01:15:25.030
Yeah, and Hyde Park, Richmond, Wimbledon, Highgate, Hampstead, most of the open spaces.

01:15:25.170 --> 01:15:25.690
You've seen them?

01:15:25.790 --> 01:15:28.030
Only Battersea. But I talked to one of the drivers.

01:15:28.090 --> 01:15:29.970
In the last few days, he delivered similar gear.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:33.420
to the others. For what? I don't know. I never got that far. The foreman came up shouting

01:15:33.420 --> 01:15:39.040
security. Any ideas? Yes. Community washing centers. Good God. Why, you got ugly neighbors?

01:15:39.800 --> 01:15:43.440
If that's right, means there'll be no more private water at all. Correct. Just turn on

01:15:43.440 --> 01:15:47.340
your taps and hear the rude noise of progress. Mrs. Davis, you got any photographers down

01:15:47.340 --> 01:15:52.160
there? Well, push them up here fast. Mitchell, get on to Glasgow office. I want a list of

01:15:52.160 --> 01:15:57.300
any similar sites in Scotland. You, Manchester. Right. Tell them to check all main towns north

01:15:57.300 --> 01:15:59.910
of Birmingham. They'll have a go with the ministries. You might surprise something.

01:16:00.060 --> 01:16:01.640
If I do, they'll surprise something out of me.

01:16:03.240 --> 01:16:05.590
And what about us messenger boys?

01:16:05.670 --> 01:16:07.110
Are we allowed to jump around a bit, too?

01:16:07.370 --> 01:16:08.310
You jumped, haven't you?

01:16:08.330 --> 01:16:09.630
You've probably brought in a hell of a beat.

01:16:10.000 --> 01:16:11.340
But I'm not capable of writing it.

01:16:11.420 --> 01:16:12.080
Oh, relax.

01:16:12.640 --> 01:16:13.660
This is Davis, sir.

01:16:14.070 --> 01:16:15.990
Standing's brought in a lead to something that could be big.

01:16:17.120 --> 01:16:19.060
Well, it looks like they're about to cut all running water.

01:16:20.340 --> 01:16:22.380
Yeah, but I'd like him to talk to you before he writes anything.

01:16:23.750 --> 01:16:26.030
Unless you need to be putting up community showers in all the parks.

01:16:27.720 --> 01:16:28.560
All right, I'll send him in.

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:29.980
Pete, go tell...

01:16:30.440 --> 01:16:32.940
Well, we're getting pictures.

01:16:33.140 --> 01:16:35.280
What about the hospitals? Well, try them.

01:16:35.540 --> 01:16:37.540
If it's going to happen, they're bound to know about it.

01:16:37.620 --> 01:16:38.760
They'll have made special arrangements.

01:16:39.580 --> 01:16:41.760
And if anything comes through on the tapes, let me know.

01:16:42.200 --> 01:16:43.480
You actually saw these showers?

01:16:43.600 --> 01:16:44.860
About 50 of them at the Battersea Hut.

01:16:45.020 --> 01:16:47.120
I wouldn't like you to make guesses about things like this.

01:16:47.220 --> 01:16:48.400
There's too much panic about as it is.

01:16:48.420 --> 01:16:50.180
Sorry, Clive McCready on the phone from Moscow.

01:16:50.340 --> 01:16:50.680
Moscow?

01:16:50.920 --> 01:16:51.360
It's urgent.

01:16:51.480 --> 01:16:52.160
Very bad line.

01:16:52.420 --> 01:16:53.780
I wonder if it got through at all.

01:16:54.500 --> 01:16:56.890
Clive? Hello? Clive?

01:16:57.900 --> 01:16:59.420
Yes, I can't hear you.

01:17:01.210 --> 01:17:06.450
Yes. Uh-huh. Yes.

01:17:06.780 --> 01:17:09.490
I've got it. I only wish I didn't believe it.

01:17:10.150 --> 01:17:12.530
I expect you'd like to come home now. Is anything still flying?

01:17:13.840 --> 01:17:17.000
Well, it's up to you. You can if you wish.

01:17:29.540 --> 01:17:29.940
Comfren!

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:35.240
Fast. Jacko, conference. Jacko, where the hell are you?

01:17:35.660 --> 01:17:36.020
Yes, sir.

01:17:36.340 --> 01:17:40.880
In here. Get a move on. Miss Evans, get the old man.

01:17:41.000 --> 01:17:42.980
I think he's at Cherkley, but wherever he is, I'll find him.

01:17:43.280 --> 01:17:44.060
You want me to wait?

01:17:54.360 --> 01:17:55.540
You have a family, don't you?

01:17:57.110 --> 01:17:57.790
I have a son.

01:18:00.090 --> 01:18:01.470
I'd get him to the country if you can.

01:18:01.710 --> 01:18:03.710
I don't think things in the cities are going to be too pleasant.

01:18:03.870 --> 01:18:05.730
I brought the dummy in case you want to tear that up.

01:18:05.850 --> 01:18:06.670
On the direct line.

01:18:09.160 --> 01:18:09.820
Good evening, sir.

01:18:10.900 --> 01:18:12.760
Well, the Russians have just about topped everything.

01:18:13.730 --> 01:18:15.150
MacReady got through from Moscow.

01:18:15.710 --> 01:18:17.590
They held an international press conference,

01:18:17.730 --> 01:18:19.110
had their top scientists present.

01:18:20.270 --> 01:18:22.990
They say that those two bangs did more than alter the tilt.

01:18:23.380 --> 01:18:25.540
They made an 11-degree shift in our orbit,

01:18:27.920 --> 01:18:29.960
and we're moving towards the sun.

01:18:32.430 --> 01:18:34.870
Well, I can't see what they'd gained by making it up.

01:18:35.680 --> 01:18:38.100
They say Western scientists have known about this all along,

01:18:38.140 --> 01:18:40.260
but were trying to work something out before they broke it.

01:18:40.740 --> 01:18:44.540
It's not through on the agencies yet, but it's bound to be any moment.

01:18:45.400 --> 01:18:46.460
No, of course not.

01:18:47.590 --> 01:18:49.550
No, that's the way I was going to play it.

01:18:50.580 --> 01:18:52.440
I'll have it put through to you.

01:18:57.220 --> 01:18:59.070
Anyone want a recap, or did you get it?

01:18:59.150 --> 01:18:59.750
We got it.

01:19:00.030 --> 01:19:05.590
the hell do we do with it? To start with, it's two lines of 120 point across eight columns. I'm not up

01:19:05.590 --> 01:19:11.820
on my sci-fi, so we're orbiting towards the sun. Well, how many billion light years... If that's true, I'd say

01:19:11.820 --> 01:19:19.480
there's about four months. Before what? Before there's a delightful smell in the universe of

01:19:19.480 --> 01:19:24.850
charcoal mankind. You really mean four months? According to the temperature rises in the last

01:19:24.850 --> 01:19:29.750
few weeks, yes. So that's the message, is it? About another 300 deadlines.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:31.460
Is that what we say in tomorrow's paper?

01:19:31.580 --> 01:19:33.480
Tomorrow we report the news, the facts.

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:35.460
We leave prophecy to the street corner cranks.

01:19:35.760 --> 01:19:39.730
Bill, you and Sandy, start arranging interviews with top scientists, Cockcroft, Penny.

01:19:40.070 --> 01:19:42.950
If you can get a call through to Calcutta, Haldane might break silence.

01:19:43.230 --> 01:19:45.510
Pete, you get busy on the no-water angle.

01:19:45.940 --> 01:19:50.380
Everything affected, sanitation, electricity, power plants, even down to car radiators.

01:19:51.200 --> 01:19:54.120
And keep the tone of the paper reasonably optimistic, understand?

01:19:54.500 --> 01:19:56.200
It's going to be all right. That's it.

01:19:57.190 --> 01:19:58.430
Sir, there's an agency flash.

01:19:58.630 --> 01:19:59.510
Yes, we just had it.

01:19:59.550 --> 01:19:59.970
In every...

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:11.820
possible way. Home, please. What does all mean? I was going on holiday next month. You think I

01:20:11.820 --> 01:20:15.280
should cancel it? The only thing that cancels is your life insurance. Set up and have an orgy. Nice public

01:20:15.280 --> 01:20:19.720
spirited advice. Oh, do me a favour. Can't do anything to be done, Mr. McGrath? Yes, there's something you can do right away.

01:20:19.880 --> 01:20:24.540
Pop off to the library. Bring me everything they've got on melting points. Melting points? Yes, the temperatures

01:20:24.540 --> 01:20:29.830
at which everything from steel to my glass eye starts to melt. Right. I suppose it couldn't be a mistake.

01:20:31.530 --> 01:20:33.940
It is. The daddy of all mistakes.

01:20:35.300 --> 01:20:37.680
Funny how when the chopper falls, everyone just accepts it.

01:20:38.400 --> 01:20:39.920
What else when it's your neck under it?

01:20:40.330 --> 01:20:43.550
The fellow here from Leeds says he's found a method of extracting water from the atmosphere.

01:20:44.520 --> 01:20:45.860
Oh, as you were, he's been certified.

01:20:47.870 --> 01:20:50.450
Jeff said to get the kid out of town, it might be a bit easier in the country.

01:20:50.670 --> 01:20:53.350
Yes, it might. Couldn't get my old woman away from the garden.

01:20:53.470 --> 01:20:54.830
She's got about six minutes of sweet peas.

01:20:54.970 --> 01:20:56.710
She'll be out buying black market water for them.

01:20:57.390 --> 01:20:59.840
Which reminds me, this is highly illegal too.

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:00.400
You want to sweep?

01:21:00.560 --> 01:21:00.940
No, thanks.

01:21:01.830 --> 01:21:02.230
Quite right.

01:21:02.470 --> 01:21:03.890
I suppose they'll do something, won't they, Bill?

01:21:04.920 --> 01:21:05.100
What?

01:21:05.660 --> 01:21:06.100
Well, something.

01:21:06.200 --> 01:21:06.880
They've got to do something.

01:21:06.980 --> 01:21:07.820
They can't just let...

01:21:08.060 --> 01:21:10.450
You've got a piece to write, haven't you?

01:21:10.510 --> 01:21:11.410
I know what I'd like to write.

01:21:11.570 --> 01:21:12.010
You can't.

01:21:12.030 --> 01:21:12.810
This is a family newspaper.

01:21:12.910 --> 01:21:14.950
We're all so bloody clever at outsmarting nature.

01:21:15.070 --> 01:21:16.630
Anything you can split, I can split better.

01:21:17.000 --> 01:21:18.040
Do you know what I'd do if I were you?

01:21:18.180 --> 01:21:18.320
What?

01:21:18.800 --> 01:21:20.600
Some special data waiting for you in the library.

01:21:20.740 --> 01:21:25.420
An hour ago, I brought her.

01:21:27.110 --> 01:21:28.370
In that case, we'll just skip the library.

01:21:28.590 --> 01:21:29.070
You'll take my...

01:21:29.070 --> 01:21:29.970
You know she hasn't...

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:31.900
Well, how the hell can she speak to you in separate rooms?

01:21:34.290 --> 01:21:38.560
Well, I need some data anyway.

01:21:40.360 --> 01:21:44.790
Yeah. We all need whatever we can get.

01:21:46.080 --> 01:21:47.280
You see, they're all cross-filed.

01:21:47.540 --> 01:21:50.680
You have to remember what names are connected with what subjects and vice versa.

01:21:51.200 --> 01:21:57.420
Any time you want any help. I'm just getting Mr. Maguire's stuff, sir.

01:22:01.400 --> 01:22:02.560
Well, thanks.

01:22:06.690 --> 01:22:06.970
Yes?

01:22:07.290 --> 01:22:08.210
I need some...

01:22:08.230 --> 01:22:13.290
Pete, I need you too.

01:22:53.520 --> 01:22:55.180
9 o'clock tonight.

01:23:00.830 --> 01:23:02.110
Pass on this information.

01:23:02.870 --> 01:23:03.530
Attention, please.

01:23:13.680 --> 01:23:13.960
Nice.

01:23:14.200 --> 01:23:14.700
An announcement.

01:23:29.360 --> 01:23:29.500
Nice.

01:23:32.300 --> 01:23:34.720
I'm sorry, sir, no entry. You'd better close this jump.

01:23:34.900 --> 01:23:37.400
I'm meeting someone inside. Not during rationales.

01:23:37.730 --> 01:23:39.810
Yeah, but anyone inside will have to come out this way.

01:23:40.090 --> 01:23:41.090
Look, don't start having a gun.

01:23:41.090 --> 01:23:43.990
It's no use arguing. You can't take those in. Leave them here.

01:23:44.270 --> 01:23:45.710
Look, I want to save half my shower.

01:23:45.830 --> 01:23:47.750
You can't take water from here. This is a washing centre.

01:23:47.950 --> 01:23:48.450
Forms, please.

01:23:48.730 --> 01:23:51.290
I don't dig it, though. I mean, like, we're all entitled to so much water, aren't we?

01:23:51.290 --> 01:23:52.980
Here for washing. Leave them here.

01:23:53.140 --> 01:23:54.820
So who says if it goes over us or in us?

01:23:54.880 --> 01:23:55.600
Move on!

01:23:55.800 --> 01:23:56.400
Stop it!

01:23:57.020 --> 01:23:57.560
Keep them!

01:24:24.800 --> 01:24:25.480
Let me in, son.

01:24:26.770 --> 01:24:28.590
We're driving away now into the country.

01:24:28.790 --> 01:24:29.290
I know, Mike.

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:32.020
You wanted us to say goodbye to the ghost train.

01:24:32.120 --> 01:24:34.060
Well, there isn't time now in any way. It's closed up.

01:24:34.440 --> 01:24:35.920
Will it be open again sometime?

01:24:36.380 --> 01:24:37.280
Yeah, maybe sometime.

01:24:38.620 --> 01:24:40.460
Mummy's here. Come and see Mummy.

01:24:41.950 --> 01:24:44.600
You can come with us. You come with us, Daddy.

01:24:45.020 --> 01:24:46.420
Make Daddy come with us, Mummy.

01:24:46.640 --> 01:24:51.720
Hello, Peter. Hello, Angie.

01:24:52.300 --> 01:24:53.760
Why can't Daddy come with us?

01:24:55.690 --> 01:24:57.910
Somebody's got to stay and report it when the ghost train starts again.

01:24:58.110 --> 01:24:59.030
We'd want to know that, wouldn't we?

01:24:59.420 --> 01:24:59.860
I suppose.

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:00.440
I suppose so.

01:25:01.920 --> 01:25:02.660
You're staying in London.

01:25:03.440 --> 01:25:04.260
The paper goes on forever.

01:25:05.340 --> 01:25:06.540
When will I see you again?

01:25:09.490 --> 01:25:09.990
Quite soon.

01:25:10.670 --> 01:25:11.390
Come out and visit you.

01:25:11.490 --> 01:25:12.130
Now go on, off you go.

01:25:12.320 --> 01:25:13.320
I want to sit him!

01:25:13.830 --> 01:25:15.350
All right, but don't wriggle the whole time.

01:25:17.700 --> 01:25:18.620
We ought to leave now, Angelo.

01:25:21.230 --> 01:25:21.450
Bye.

01:25:21.750 --> 01:25:22.730
Bye, Angie.

01:25:27.210 --> 01:25:28.210
You got water in those cans?

01:25:28.530 --> 01:25:29.250
We saved a little.

01:25:29.570 --> 01:25:29.970
Now keep to the...

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:34.380
main roads. The water gangs are pretty busy. I won't take chances. Well, good luck being

01:25:34.380 --> 01:25:41.400
in. It seems pretty ridiculous now, doesn't it? Yeah, pretty ridiculous. Look after the

01:25:41.400 --> 01:25:46.060
kid. See you. Maybe.

01:26:00.410 --> 01:26:00.730
important.

01:26:41.290 --> 01:26:42.130
Try and get it clearer.

01:26:42.430 --> 01:26:43.330
Nothing much I can do, sir.

01:26:43.450 --> 01:26:44.610
We're using every known cutout.

01:26:44.830 --> 01:26:45.270
What do you bet?

01:26:45.370 --> 01:26:46.910
They'll have another power failure in the middle of it.

01:26:47.010 --> 01:26:47.530
It's all right, sir.

01:26:47.590 --> 01:26:48.450
We're running on our own generation.

01:26:48.490 --> 01:26:50.030
We'll have to wait for agency copy.

01:26:50.170 --> 01:26:51.790
Ronnie, hop over to PA and wait for it.

01:26:53.600 --> 01:26:54.080
Go on.

01:27:05.500 --> 01:27:06.540
Keep it quiet, please.

01:27:11.380 --> 01:27:21.200
It's only taken half a million years.

01:27:22.210 --> 01:27:23.310
He's been shaking like a jelly.

01:27:24.280 --> 01:27:25.900
Well, with his tongue, it's all swollen.

01:27:26.420 --> 01:27:27.440
All right, try and get a doctor.

01:28:34.190 --> 01:28:35.370
You just missed the good news.

01:28:35.490 --> 01:28:36.310
I heard it at the PA.

01:28:36.550 --> 01:28:37.910
Here's the full speech, official release.

01:28:38.050 --> 01:28:38.930
When's the Big Bang?

01:28:39.090 --> 01:28:40.090
Wednesday, 11 a.m.

01:28:40.310 --> 01:28:41.790
They've got to have their tea break first, you know.

01:28:42.190 --> 01:28:43.190
I don't think that's humorous.

01:28:43.290 --> 01:28:44.390
Don't you? Well, try this for a laugh.

01:28:44.510 --> 01:28:46.190
There's five cases of typhus in North London

01:28:46.190 --> 01:28:47.170
and they're expecting more.

01:28:47.430 --> 01:28:47.750
Typhus?

01:28:47.950 --> 01:28:49.350
Yes, from black market water, mostly.

01:28:49.570 --> 01:28:50.350
Four pounds a gallon.

01:28:50.450 --> 01:28:51.430
Typhus, no extra charge.

01:28:51.920 --> 01:28:52.480
What happened, kid?

01:28:52.780 --> 01:28:54.020
Don't touch many of you. Keep away.

01:28:55.150 --> 01:28:55.710
Where's he live?

01:28:55.850 --> 01:28:57.010
Live? I don't know. North London, sir.

01:28:57.650 --> 01:28:58.290
That's what I thought.

01:28:58.940 --> 01:28:59.480
Call emergency.

01:29:00.000 --> 01:29:00.980
An ambulance. I'll get it.

01:29:01.640 --> 01:29:04.340
Poor little bastard. Well, this is all we needed.

01:29:04.990 --> 01:29:07.290
We'll have to see your staff's inoculated before anyone leaves.

01:29:07.450 --> 01:29:09.070
Of course. Well, at least we have a choice now.

01:29:09.250 --> 01:29:10.270
Typhus or leukemia.

01:29:10.580 --> 01:29:12.770
What do you think, Bill? The bombs, I mean.

01:29:12.910 --> 01:29:16.530
You heard what the PM said? No one. Not even Bill McGuire can answer that one.

01:29:16.810 --> 01:29:17.830
And if it doesn't work?

01:29:18.250 --> 01:29:20.550
Evacuation by spaceship. All aboard for the moon.

01:29:20.690 --> 01:29:22.290
You want the best dead planets? We are them.

01:29:22.470 --> 01:29:22.990
What a slug.

01:29:23.800 --> 01:29:24.100
Peace.

01:29:24.610 --> 01:29:27.410
If that's your last will and testament, don't forget to leave me my car.

01:29:27.610 --> 01:29:29.970
I'm de-gutting the polluted water peddlers. Give us my...

01:29:31.120 --> 01:29:34.540
Plus the 12 pounds I paid for my water circulator I haven't even seen.

01:29:35.160 --> 01:29:36.430
You want some? I'm off it.

01:29:36.890 --> 01:29:39.870
So I've noticed. Beautiful thing to watch a woman reform a man.

01:29:40.030 --> 01:29:41.410
Only needed the earth to catch fire.

01:29:41.590 --> 01:29:42.230
Glad you approve.

01:29:42.590 --> 01:29:44.750
Well, if you've exhausted your repertoire of amusing quips,

01:29:44.790 --> 01:29:46.490
I'll go and collect the other half of the reform movement.

01:29:46.670 --> 01:29:48.100
She's gone. Gone?

01:29:48.730 --> 01:29:50.510
Yeah, she'd collected enough water for a bath

01:29:50.510 --> 01:29:52.490
and she was going to have it before you turned it into coffee.

01:29:53.030 --> 01:29:53.690
Can I use the car?

01:29:54.030 --> 01:29:55.550
Surely. I've forgotten how to drive it.

01:30:04.190 --> 01:30:07.960
There you are, Jacko. Read all about it, including the carbon. Slash what you want. I'm going home.

01:30:08.020 --> 01:30:10.390
Home? Yes, I've done enough work for one lifetime.

01:30:10.490 --> 01:30:12.650
But you have to be injected. Against what? The end of the world?

01:30:23.930 --> 01:30:26.350
Keep moving, please. Down to the embankment. The road's closed.

01:30:26.530 --> 01:30:29.270
Can I get back on at the other end? Not tonight. This district's out of bounds.

01:30:30.500 --> 01:30:32.640
No, there's some teenage kids kicking it up a bit.

01:30:32.990 --> 01:30:34.790
They lit a few fires, looted a bit of water.

01:30:35.360 --> 01:30:37.300
Where are you going, sir? Embankment Terrace.

01:30:37.800 --> 01:30:39.040
Okay, let this one through. Thanks.

01:30:39.440 --> 01:30:40.840
If you see any of them, keep driving.

01:30:41.040 --> 01:30:42.720
They're either drunk or drunk. Great party.

01:30:42.960 --> 01:30:45.280
And stay clear of Chelsea. They say it's pretty rough down there.

01:30:45.320 --> 01:30:46.420
It always was, wasn't it?

01:31:46.160 --> 01:31:48.130
think you're doing. We're making a great

01:31:48.130 --> 01:31:50.270
clean end, man. Real cool, clean

01:31:50.270 --> 01:31:50.670
finish.

01:32:14.650 --> 01:32:16.630
Throw this lot over at the stinky little cat.

01:32:17.130 --> 01:32:18.650
That's what you've been saving up for, darling.

01:32:18.950 --> 01:32:19.330
Give me that.

01:32:20.900 --> 01:32:21.880
All right, that's enough.

01:32:22.020 --> 01:32:22.500
Now get out.

01:32:25.210 --> 01:32:29.860
We're taking off the last of the bath together.

01:32:59.850 --> 01:32:59.930
Thank you.

01:33:01.950 --> 01:33:03.340
No, it's nothing. You?

01:33:03.670 --> 01:33:15.280
All this time I hit something and barricade that door.

01:33:19.760 --> 01:33:20.920
They've all gone insane.

01:33:21.040 --> 01:33:21.940
Yes, the new fashion.

01:33:24.120 --> 01:33:26.060
Did you hear the speech?

01:33:28.130 --> 01:33:28.750
I heard.

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:31.440
Why did it all have to happen, Pete?

01:33:31.720 --> 01:33:37.550
It just had to, that's all.

01:33:37.710 --> 01:33:40.150
You're too smart.

01:33:41.040 --> 01:33:42.460
All people want to do is live.

01:33:42.680 --> 01:33:43.440
I don't think so.

01:33:44.620 --> 01:33:45.780
I wish I could understand why.

01:33:46.140 --> 01:33:47.260
A lot of people don't want to live.

01:33:47.420 --> 01:33:47.980
It's too difficult.

01:33:48.960 --> 01:33:50.040
They're tired, they're frightened.

01:33:50.760 --> 01:33:53.570
I'd rather it was all over than go on worrying, being frightened,

01:33:54.560 --> 01:33:56.100
losing a bit more hope every day.

01:33:57.020 --> 01:33:57.860
So they wanted to finish.

01:33:59.130 --> 01:33:59.830
But I don't.

01:34:00.800 --> 01:34:01.800
I don't want it to.

01:34:02.670 --> 01:34:03.470
Neither do I.

01:34:05.240 --> 01:34:05.480
Now.

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:35.140
You are advised to stay until 10.47.

01:34:37.310 --> 01:34:38.830
The time is now 10.51.

01:34:49.930 --> 01:34:58.010
The boys stood on the burning deck whence all but he had fled.

01:34:58.090 --> 01:34:59.970
My children, they fled to the machine room when...

01:35:00.000 --> 01:35:00.700
Where the booze is hidden.

01:35:01.130 --> 01:35:01.970
But not the deck boy?

01:35:02.440 --> 01:35:03.460
He has an assignment.

01:35:04.070 --> 01:35:04.310
See you.

01:35:06.880 --> 01:35:07.460
Where are you going?

01:35:08.400 --> 01:35:10.160
To see what happens after the man says zero.

01:35:10.980 --> 01:35:12.760
Eyewitness account of whether we're coming or just when.

01:35:12.800 --> 01:35:14.680
Bill, let me cover it.

01:35:15.000 --> 01:35:15.400
No, Pete.

01:35:17.250 --> 01:35:18.510
Take it home to bed, you idiot.

01:35:21.160 --> 01:35:23.960
We can make it to Harry's and down it in three and a half minutes.

01:35:24.120 --> 01:35:24.760
I'll buy you both a drink.

01:35:28.900 --> 01:35:29.740
I got something then.

01:35:30.570 --> 01:35:32.390
I can't think what you want to listen in for, anyway.

01:35:32.650 --> 01:35:34.210
It's a historic occasion, May.

01:35:34.390 --> 01:35:36.190
It's like the Queen's speech on Christmas Day.

01:35:36.410 --> 01:35:37.930
It's a lot of numbers, that's all you'll hear.

01:35:38.500 --> 01:35:40.560
I can't see what's historic about someone counting.

01:35:40.880 --> 01:35:41.840
All right, open the safe.

01:35:41.960 --> 01:35:42.800
What have you got left, May?

01:35:43.230 --> 01:35:44.470
Oh, I'm so glad.

01:35:44.630 --> 01:35:45.990
It's so nice to have someone in the club.

01:35:46.890 --> 01:35:47.250
Exactly.

01:35:47.510 --> 01:35:48.210
Thirty seconds.

01:35:49.470 --> 01:35:50.130
Thirty seconds.

01:35:51.380 --> 01:35:54.460
I've been saving this, and this one's on the house.

01:35:54.660 --> 01:35:55.580
Take it easy now, May.

01:35:55.720 --> 01:35:56.980
That half-bottle cost a fortune.

01:35:57.660 --> 01:35:59.980
I ever got to be manageress to a mean old bastard...

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:01.040
Like you, I'll never know.

01:36:01.640 --> 01:36:03.280
You've never said anything like that before.

01:36:03.460 --> 01:36:05.500
No, but I've thought it, and I've said it, and I'm glad.

01:36:05.860 --> 01:36:06.340
There's yours.

01:36:07.700 --> 01:36:09.200
Countdown must have started by now.

01:36:10.050 --> 01:36:10.630
Drink up, then.

01:36:13.520 --> 01:36:13.920
Here's how.

01:36:15.100 --> 01:36:20.310
The luck of the human race.

01:36:32.010 --> 01:36:36.330
We need children of this.

01:36:46.260 --> 01:36:48.480
St. Louis, count. Must be about nine.

01:36:49.700 --> 01:36:49.940
Seven.

01:36:50.870 --> 01:36:51.790
Always later than you think.

01:36:55.080 --> 01:36:55.360
May.

01:36:57.060 --> 01:36:57.340
I'm here.

01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:01.060
What do you mean all that biffle?

01:37:03.740 --> 01:37:04.560
Silly old bird.

01:37:04.840 --> 01:37:05.100
No.

01:37:08.570 --> 01:37:08.730
Let me.

01:37:18.120 --> 01:37:18.940
Go on, you bloody idiot.

01:37:27.130 --> 01:37:29.860
A man has sown the wind and reaped the whirlwind.

01:37:31.310 --> 01:37:52.280
in the next few hours there will be no remembrance of the past and no hope for the future that might have been all the works of man will be consumed in the great fire out of which he was created but perhaps at the heart of the burning light into which he has thrust his world there is a heart that cares more for him than he has ever cared for himself

01:38:25.530 --> 01:38:28.560
future sensitive as he is

01:38:30.220 --> 01:38:46.800
defiant in his pursuit of power let him resolve to live it lovingly for he knows well how to do so then he may say once more truly the light is sweet pleasant thing it is for the eyes to see the sun
